          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em smooth groupoid
            is the first generalization of the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"}> smooth spaces
            ) to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"}> smooth manifolds
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deloopings"} deloopings
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+groups"} diffeological groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
          %p
            Technically, a smooth groupoid is a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            (sometimes called a “smooth stack”, but this is somewhat ambiguous), or equivalently on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subsite"} dense subsite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            of just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            between them. This is equivalently an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truncated+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} 1-truncated
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth ∞-groupoid
          %p
            For more see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+homotopy+types"} geometry of physics – smooth homotopy types
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong on how not to define smooth groupoids
            %p
              One could be tempted to define smooth groupoids to be
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoids"} internal groupoids
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
              yields the concept of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+groupoid"} diffeological groupoid
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              yields the concept of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
              %em not
              in themselves exhibit the correct
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              of smooth groupoids. One may fix this by changing the concept of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              to generalized morphisms called
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+morphisms"} Morita morphisms
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bibundles"} bibundles
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bibundles"} bibundles
              between groupoids its correct generalization to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+2-groupoids"} smooth 2-groupoids
              etc.
            %p
              But just as the definition of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              = succeed "," do
                %em simpler
            %p
              Now, this definition, given below, amounts to saying that smooth groupoids are stacks on the site of smooth manifolds, and that in turn may tend to not sound like a simple definition at all. But there is a further simplification at work. Traditional texts tend to define stacks in terms of the comparatively intricate structures of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctors"} pseudofunctors
              or (dually under the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+categories"} fibered categories
              satisfying
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
              of categories turns out to handle the same theory of stacks by much more tractable means (e.g.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "stack#Hollander01"} Hollander 01
              %em presented
              by a plain functor with no
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
              condition imposed, something that is hence as simple as a pair of two presheaves. The nature of stacks is then instead just encoded in remembering that some of the morphisms of presheaves of groupoids are to be labeled as
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %p
              This style of definition combines the simplicity of the naive definition of Lie groupoids with the full power of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
              which is just as simple, hence, in the present context, to a definition of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth ∞-groupoid
          %h3#PreSmoothGroupoids Pre-smooth groupoids
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Write
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              between them. (The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothMfd"} SmoothMfd
              on the Cartesian spaces.) Regard this as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
              by equipping it with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
              of (differentiably)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+covers"} good open covers
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+coordinate+systems"} geometry of physics – coordinate systems
          %p
            In view of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"} motivation for sheaves, cohomology and higher stacks
            and in direct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogy
            with the discussion at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} geometry of physics – smooth sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            throughout, we set:
          #PreSmoothGroupoid.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em pre-smooth groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi CartSp
                      %mi op
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \colon CartSp^{op} \longrightarrow Grpd
            %p
              from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+homotopy+types#Groupoids"} geometry of physics – homotopy types – groupoids
            for more on bare groupoids. Here we will freely assume familiarity with these.
          /
            \-
            Added this because otherwise the website says no content was changed
            \-
          #YonedaIntuition.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The intuition for def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PreSmoothGroupoid"}
              is the following: a smooth structure on a groupoid is to be determined by which maps from abstract
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+systems"} coordinate systems
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              into it are to be regarded as smooth maps. Since, by its groupoidal nature, two such maps may be related by a smooth
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
              (in physics: “twisted sectors”), the collection of all smooth functions from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              into the smooth groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is itself a bare groupoid. We here
              %em define
              a pre-smooth groupoid as something that assigns to each abstract coordinate systems a groupoid of would-be smooth maps into it
            %p
              “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↗
                              %msup
                                %mo ↘
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mi smooth
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mi ℝ
                                %mi n
                            %mtd
                              %mo ⇓
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↘
                              %msub
                                %mo ↗
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mi smooth
                      %mo }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X(\mathbb{R}^n) = \left\{ \array{
                    \&amp; \nearrow \searrow^{\mathrlap{smooth}}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{R}^n
                    \&amp;\Downarrow&amp;
                    X
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \searrow \nearrow_{\mathrlap{smooth}}
                    } \right\}
              ”
            %p
              We sometimes therefore speak of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(\mathbb{R}^n)
              as the groupoid of
              %em plots
              or of
              %em probes
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (which here is only defined by these!) by
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            will turn this intuition into a theorem. For that we need to speak of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            of pre-smooth groupoids, hence of “smooth functors” betwen pre-smooth groupoids.
          #HomGroupoidsOfPreSmoothGroupoids.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              or
              %em smooth map
              between pre-smooth groupoids is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
              that they are. We write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PreSmooth
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Type
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi Funct
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi CartSp
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    PreSmooth1Type
                    \\coloneqq
                    Funct(CartSp^{op}, Grpd)
            %p
              for the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                  of pre-smooth groupoids
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PreSmoothGroupoid"}
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            %p
              This means that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi PreSmooth
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y \in PreSmooth1Type
              two pre-smooth groupoids, then the bare
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-groupoid"} hom-groupoid
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PreSmooth
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Type
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    PreSmooth1Type(X,Y)_\bullet
                    \\in
                    Grpd
            %p between them has
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  as
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
                  between
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                  regarded as
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f(\mathbb{R}^n)\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                  between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
                  of probes
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        f(\mathbb{R}^n) \colon X(\mathbb{R}^n) \longrightarrow Y(\mathbb{R}^n)
                %p
                  such that for every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
                  between abstract coordinate charts, these form a (strictly)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
                  with the probe-pullback functors of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  and
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi X
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msup
                                  %mi ℝ
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi n
                                      %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo ⟶
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi f
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %msup
                                      %mi ℝ
                                      %mi n
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %mi Y
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msup
                                  %mi ℝ
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi n
                                      %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi X
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi ϕ
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi Y
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi ϕ
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi X
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msup
                                  %mi ℝ
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi n
                                      %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo ⟶
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi f
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %msup
                                      %mi ℝ
                                      %mi n
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %mi Y
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msup
                                  %mi ℝ
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi n
                                      %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ;
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        X(\mathbb{R}^{n_1}) &amp;\overset{f(\mathbb{R}^n)}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Y(\mathbb{R}^{n_1})
                        \\\
                        {}^{\mathllap{X(\phi)}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{Y(\phi)}}
                        \\\
                        X(\mathbb{R}^{n_2}) &amp;\overset{f(\mathbb{R}^n)}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Y(\mathbb{R}^{n_2})
                        }
                        \\,;
              %li
                %p
                  as
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi f
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \colon f \rightarrow g
                  the “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modifications"}> modifications
                  ” of these, hence collections
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(\mathbb{R}^n) \colon f(\mathbb{R}^n)\to g(\mathbb{R}^n)
                  of
